linkrd -- building the Debian source package
============================================

Layout
------

  debian/                        Debian packaging (this directory)
  man/linkrd.1                   upstream man page, shared with other packagers
  packaging/deb/build-deb.sh     generates prebuilt .deb files on macOS
  packaging/deb/validate-deb.py  structural validator for those .deb files
  packaging/deb/README.md        notes about the prebuilt packages

The Debian packaging was added by the packager.  No upstream source file is
modified (no patches are applied, debian/patches/ is absent).  The udev rule
and the systemd unit are the files from contrib/, referenced through symlinks
in debian/ so that there is exactly one copy in the tree:

  debian/linkrd.udev          -> contrib/udev/70-linkrd.rules
  debian/linkrd.user.service  -> contrib/systemd/linkrd.service

The .user.service suffix is how debhelper 13 installs systemd *user* units:
dh_installsystemd only handles system units, while dh_installsystemduser reads
debian/package.user.service and installs it as
/usr/lib/systemd/user/package.service.  (dh_installsystemd has no --user
option; older packaging instructions that mention one are wrong.)

Building
--------

Install the build dependencies and run, from the top of the source tree:

  dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

or, for a single binary package without signing:

  debuild -us -uc -b

Requirements:
  * cargo and rustc >= 1.89.  Rust edition 2024 needs >= 1.85, but the
    code also uses std::fs::File::lock/try_lock (stabilised in 1.89) and
    the locked "time" crate requires 1.88, so 1.89 is the real MSRV.
    Debian 13 (trixie) ships rustc 1.85.1 and therefore cannot build
    this package; build it on sid (rustc >= 1.89), with a backported
    toolchain, or with rustup in a clean chroot.
  * Network access during override_dh_auto_configure, which runs
    "cargo fetch --locked", or a pre-populated CARGO_HOME and/or a vendor
    configuration in .cargo/config.toml.  The crates are not vendored in the
    upstream tree, so a fully offline build only works with such a setup.
    The build itself always runs "cargo build --release --locked --offline".
  * No C toolchain, protoc, libudev or Wayland/X11 development packages are
    needed: clipboard-rs uses the pure-Rust wayland-backend and x11rb, and
    serialport is built with default-features = false (no libudev).
  * CARGO_HOME points at debian/cargo-home inside the build tree, so the user's
    ~/.cargo is not modified and the build is reproducible on a clean tree.

Build options
-------------

  DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nocheck   skip the cargo test suite (alias: "nocheck" in
                              DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS; the override checks it
                              explicitly and debhelper 13 also skips
                              dh_auto_test).
  DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nostrip   keep debug symbols; debhelper 13 skips
                              dh_strip/dh_dwz, and the explicit override in
                              debian/rules does the same.

The source package uses the 3.0 (quilt) format.  Because upstream does not
publish release tarballs yet (and has no tags), an upload would have to create
the .orig.tar.gz from the git tree at the packaged commit.

Licensing
---------

Upstream ships no LICENSE file and Cargo.toml has no "license" field at version
0.1.0.  debian/copyright therefore records "License: unknown" and explains why.
This package must not be uploaded to the Debian archive until upstream fixes
that; it is provided for direct installation only.

Maintainer-script policy
------------------------

The package never enables or starts the linkrd systemd user unit.  Enabling is
done once per desktop user ("systemctl --user enable --now linkrd.service"),
because the unit needs the user's clipboard, serial-port ACL and configuration.
dh_installsystemduser is called with --no-enable for that reason.  The udev
rule is a plain file under /usr/lib/udev/rules.d; dh_installudev is used with
--priority=70 so that it is installed as 70-linkrd.rules.  debhelper 13.14 and
newer install it below /usr/lib/udev/rules.d; debhelper 13.11 (Debian 12)
installs /lib/udev/rules.d, which is the same directory on merged-/usr systems.

Prebuilt binary packages
------------------------

packaging/deb/build-deb.sh creates ready-to-install .deb files without
dpkg-deb (the packager's build host is macOS).  Those packages are statically
linked against musl and only install files below /usr; they contain:

  /usr/bin/linkrd
  /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70-linkrd.rules
  /usr/lib/systemd/user/linkrd.service
  /usr/share/man/man1/linkrd.1.gz
  /usr/share/doc/linkrd/{README.md,README.zh-CN.md,README.debian,copyright,
                         changelog.Debian.gz}

Their postinst reloads the udev rules conditionally (udevadm may be absent in
containers) and does not touch systemd.  Run packaging/deb/validate-deb.py to
check the container/tar structure, md5sums, permissions and installed size.
See packaging/deb/README.md and packaging/deb/SMOKE.md for the checks that
still require a real Debian/Ubuntu machine (dpkg -i, lintian, systemd user
unit, real hardware).
